17592186420845 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186420813 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420845 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186420821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420845 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186420836 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420845 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420845 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420845 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186420804 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420845 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "db1d1fba-e4ba-50df-bd35-d24e24ca86ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420847 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420847 #edb/ref 1069 "q-42578" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420847 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420847 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $det({A}^{-1}),$ if $A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186420847 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0b58d794-d6a1-55fe-88d5-660b59bdd30a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420847 #edb/ref 1074 "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(A)}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $det(A):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =20-18 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\ndet({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(A)}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186420851 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420851 #edb/ref 1069 "q-88306" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420851 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420851 #edb/ref 1071 "$|\\begin{aligned}300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 300 & 270 & 100 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420851 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "80589b9c-bfb0-57bc-8e70-de307d877343" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420851 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that the $1$st row of our determinant is almost equal to the $2$nd row. So, we can rewrite the given determinant, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 300 & 270 & 100 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 300+0 & 250+20 & 100+0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 300+0 & 250+20 & 100+0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 0 & 20 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first determinant must be zero since it has two identical rows, while the second determinant can be expanded across the $2$nd row, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 0 & 20 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =0+20(-1)^{2+2}|\\begin{aligned}300 & 100 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =20\\cdot (300\\cdot 1-100\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =20\\cdot 200 \\\\  & =4\\,000\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420852 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420843 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420852 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130693" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420852 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420852 #edb/ref 1071 "Find\n$|\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 114 & 210 & 198 \\\\ 113 & 210 & 198\\end{aligned}|=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420852 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8c3dc4f1-ad56-595b-a7eb-41a85271c7fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420852 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that the $2$nd row of our determinant is almost equal to the $3$rd row. So, we can rewrite the given determinant, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 114 & 210 & 198 \\\\ 113 & 210 & 198\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 113+1 & 210+0 & 198+0 \\\\ 113 & 210 & 198\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 113+1 & 210+0 & 198+0 \\\\ 113 & 210 & 198\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 113 & 210 & 198 \\\\ 113 & 210 & 198\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 113 & 210 & 198\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first determinant must be zero since it has two identical rows, while the second determinant can be expanded across the $2$nd row, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 113 & 210 & 198 \\\\ 113 & 210 & 198\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 113 & 210 & 198\\end{aligned}| & =0+1(-1)^{2+1}|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 210 & 198\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(4\\cdot 198-2\\cdot 210) \\\\  & =-(792-420) \\\\  & =-372\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420853 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420853 #edb/ref 1069 "q-43070" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420853 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420853 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $det({A}^{-1}),$ if $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186420853 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9e9ddda3-97aa-551d-9453-cae76713ec59" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420853 #edb/ref 1074 "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(A)}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $det(A):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =10-5 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\ndet({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(A)}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186420854 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420854 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130474" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420854 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420854 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $C=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 5 \\\\ -6 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix},$ find $det(C).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186420854 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b1079762-fa86-56ef-a06d-de94245b17fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420854 #edb/ref 1074 "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\ndet(C)=det\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot det\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 5 \\\\ -6 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the required determinants as follows:\n\n- For the first determinant, we can compute it using a Laplace expansion across the $3$rd row.\n\n- For the second determinant, we can compute it using a Laplace expansion down the $2$nd column.\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(C) & =\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 & 5 \\\\ -6 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot (-1)(-1)^{2+2}\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(6-4)\\cdot (-1)(12-5) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-7) \\\\  & =-28.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186428341 #edb/ref 1030 1.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428341 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account linear: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: linear=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret colo